{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00169 - MONDO_2319",
  "verbalization": "Cholecalciferol is implicated in the development of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease through a series of biochemical pathways that involve hypercalcemia. The evidence suggests that Cholecalciferol is associated with Hypercalcemia Disease, which in turn is linked to specific calcium salts such as Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glucoheptonate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate. These calcium compounds are indicated to contribute to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, implying a potential role in disrupting normal phosphorus homeostasis. The shared mechanism of hypercalcemia and the involvement of calcium salts suggest a broader pathway where elevated calcium levels may influence phosphorus metabolism, possibly through interference with cellular processes or enzyme activity. The consistent association across different pathways reinforces the hypothesis that Cholecalciferol may influence phosphorus metabolism via these calcium-dependent mechanisms.",
